#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""memo-verify-provenance — MEASURE which model wrote the vectors. [002/FR-108]

Do not trust `document_chunks.embedding_model`. Measure.

SCOPE (widened 2026-08-02): BOTH vector tables — `chunk_embeddings` (passage path)
and `document_embeddings` (whole-document path). It checked only the first for its
first several weeks and ran green throughout, while the whole-document path was
regressing. A green result is scoped to what it sampled, and this one did not say
what it sampled.

**Why, concretely.** On 2026-08-02 that column read
`openai/text-embedding-3-large` for all 19,554 chunks whose vectors had actually
been written by `qwen3-embedding-4b` — the re-embed wrote the vector tables
directly and never updated the provenance. The field was present and LYING, which
is strictly worse than absent because every future reader, and every check built
on it, would trust it.

**A declared provenance column cannot check itself.** It is written by the same
code path that writes the vectors, so it inherits that path's bugs and fails in
the direction that looks fine. This uses a DIFFERENT generator: re-embed the
stored source text with the currently configured model and compare to the vector
on disk.

    cosine ~1.0  → the configured model IS the model that wrote this vector
    cosine far   → the record is lying, whatever the column says

⚠️ Threshold is 0.999, not equality, and NOT a hash. Embedding endpoints are
mildly nondeterministic under load — a real chunk measured 0.999916 while three
migrations shared the GPU — and a hash comparison would flag that as a mismatch
it isn't. Validate under load, not on an idle box. (Technique from the
`embeddings` seat's endpoint identity canary, pointed at a corpus instead.)

⚠️ **WHAT THE THRESHOLD ACTUALLY HAS TO CLEAR — MEASURED HERE, 2026-08-02.**
Run `memo-calibrate-drift` for the current figures; as of 03:10 EDT that day, on
this corpus against this endpoint (worst across 3 rounds × 16 chunks, under GPU
contention):

    singleton vs stored   1.18e-04
    batch-16  vs stored   1.15e-04
    singleton vs batch-16 1.18e-04

**0.999 clears the worst by 8.5×.** Four of the sixteen chunks differ from stored
in every round at byte-identical values, singleton and batched alike — the
endpoint does not reproduce today what it wrote for those chunks. That is the
component this threshold really has to survive.

⚠️ **Do not read one round.** Round 1 alone showed singleton-vs-batch at exactly
1.0 on 15 of 16 rows and would have supported "call shape contributes nothing";
round 2 refuted it. Which rows move is partly a lottery, so a single run of this
measurement can land anywhere from 0.000000 to ~1.7e-04.

⛔ **An earlier version of this comment claimed batch composition contributed
~1.2e-3 and that 0.999 had to absorb it. That figure was measured on
OpenRouter/text-embedding-3-large — a different inference stack — and is ~14×
larger than anything this endpoint produces.** I imported another service's
constant and wrote it here as though it described ours. It does not. (The
conclusion below was unaffected; only the stated reason was wrong.)

⇒ **The consequence that outlives the correction:** the floor is set by how much
the serving environment has changed since the vectors were written, and that grows
on its own with no event to mark it. Re-run `memo-calibrate-drift` after any GPU,
driver, or serving change — an eroding margin produces no symptom until it
produces a false PROVENANCE MISMATCH on a healthy corpus.

Singleton re-embedding is the deliberate choice, for two reasons:
  - it is the WORST-CASE composition mismatch against a batched writer, so a pass
    is conservative rather than flattering;
  - it removes the pairing hazard entirely. A batched verifier must sort by
    `.index` and verify contiguity, because a short or reordered response
    compares vectors to the WRONG CHUNKS and manufactures a provenance failure
    out of its own plumbing. Given how many real instances turned up on
    2026-08-02, a false one would be believed. A verification tool that can
    false-alarm from its own plumbing is worse than none.

The cost is that samples run serially: ~4–8 min at n=100 under contention, versus
~96s batched. That is an acceptable price for a weekly sweep, and it buys a
comparison whose only moving part is the model.

    docker compose exec -T memo-v2 python /app/scripts/memo-verify-provenance
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import asyncio
import math
import struct
import sys
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1] / "src"))
sys.path.insert(0, "/app/src")

from memo import db, embeddings  # noqa: E402
from memo.config import settings  # noqa: E402

# ⚠️ THIS NUMBER IS SCOPED TO qwen3-embedding-4b ON THE LOCAL vLLM ENDPOINT.
#
# It is not a general provenance floor, and it reads like one. It was derived by
# `embeddings` on qwen3/vLLM and re-derived HERE by measurement — worst deviation
# 1.18e-04 over 3 rounds of `memo-calibrate-drift`, so 0.999 clears the local floor
# by ~8.5×. That validation is what makes it usable here and is exactly what does
# not travel.
#
# ⛔ DO NOT INHERIT IT INTO A CHECK AGAINST A DIFFERENT STACK. `mind` measured
# OpenRouter/text-embedding-3-large at n=100 and found min 0.998772 — 1 row in 100
# of a CORRECT corpus falls below 0.999 there. memo v1 (:8000) is
# text-embedding-3-small @1536 via OpenRouter, a pair nobody has measured at all.
# Pointing this script at v1 with this default would be expected to produce false
# PROVENANCE MISMATCHes, and after 2026-08-02 a flag from this tool would be
# believed. Measure the floor on the stack you are checking; do not import it.
#
# ⛔ AND THE VALIDATION ATTACHES TO THIS VALUE, NOT TO "THE THRESHOLD". Tightening
# to 0.9995 would not inherit the measurement above — it would need its own, and
# on a corpus sitting ~1.2e-3 from the line it would start flagging healthy rows
# while the old "we checked it and it works" kept being quoted as support.
THRESHOLD = 0.999

# ⛔ A SINGLE ROW BELOW THRESHOLD IS NOT EVIDENCE OF A WRONG MODEL.
#
# The original verdict logic was `worst < threshold -> MISMATCH, exit 2`. That is
# brittle wherever the margin is small, and the margin is PROVIDER-DEPENDENT:
# `mind` measured OpenRouter/3-large at n=100 and found 1 healthy row below 0.999.
# ⇒ On that stack the old logic would call MISMATCH on a CORRECT corpus for any
# sample of ~100. Latent here (qwen worst 0.999930, ~8.5× margin), live the moment
# this points at anything else. (`mind` hit it live; `embeddings` relayed.)
#
# ⭐ THE BAND IS DEFINED BY THE NOISE OF THE SIGNAL, NOT THE NOISE OF THE NULL.
# These sound like one idea and are two different measurements:
#     noise of the NULL   — how HIGH can an UNRELATED pair score?  (the control)
#     noise of the SIGNAL — how LOW can a CORRECT pair score?      (this)
# `mind`'s first fix used the control's maximum as the divider — elegant and
# self-calibrating and the wrong quantity: a row at 0.62 against a control topping
# out at 0.4 came back "marginal" when it is plainly anomalous.
#
# So this floor comes from observed CORRECT-pair deviation, across every stack
# anyone has measured:
#     qwen3/vLLM here          worst 1.18e-04 below 1.0  (memo-calibrate-drift, 3 rounds)
#     OpenRouter/3-large       worst 1.23e-03 below 1.0  (`mind`, n=100)
# 5e-3 is ~4× the largest of those. A row inside [MARGINAL_FLOOR, THRESHOLD) is
# near the line; a row below MARGINAL_FLOOR is nowhere near it and means another
# model wrote that vector.
#
# ⚠️ MARGINAL_FLOOR DECAYS FOR THE SAME REASON THRESHOLD DOES — it is the same
# quantity expressed as a width instead of a point. Re-run `memo-calibrate-drift`
# after any GPU, driver, or serving change and revisit BOTH, or one quietly
# outlives the other.
MARGINAL_FLOOR = 0.995

# If more than this fraction of a sample lands in the marginal band, the finding is
# about the THRESHOLD, not about the corpus — a different diagnosis with a different
# exit code, so the two cannot be confused in a log.
MARGINAL_FRACTION_LIMIT = 0.05


def classify(c: float, threshold: float, marginal_floor: float) -> str:
    """ok | marginal | mismatch — three states, because two cannot say 'near the line'."""
    if c >= threshold:
        return "ok"
    if c >= marginal_floor:
        return "marginal"
    return "mismatch"


def verdict(rows: list[tuple[str, float]], threshold: float, marginal_floor: float,
            fraction_limit: float = MARGINAL_FRACTION_LIMIT) -> tuple[int, str]:
    """Turn per-row classifications into an exit code and a sentence.

    Returned rather than printed so every branch can be exercised in a test. A
    verdict path that has never been watched to fire is not a check.
    """
    n = len(rows)
    if not n:
        return 0, "nothing checked"
    bad = [(k, c) for k, c in rows if classify(c, threshold, marginal_floor) == "mismatch"]
    marg = [(k, c) for k, c in rows if classify(c, threshold, marginal_floor) == "marginal"]
    if bad:
        worst = min(c for _, c in bad)
        rate = len(bad) / n
        # ⚠️ `if bad:` is a MIN in disguise — drawing more rows can only increase the
        # chance of catching one, so the gate gets stricter the more carefully you
        # sample. It is not currently exposed: measured healthy drift is 1.18e-04, so
        # a healthy row sits at ~0.9999 and MARGINAL_FLOOR is ~40x outside anything
        # observed. But the margin is the same quantity that decays with a serving
        # change, and the rule is worth honouring before it bites.
        #
        # The real defect was the MESSAGE, not the branch. One bad row IS a real
        # provenance failure for that row, so exit 2 is right either way — but saying
        # "these vectors were written by a different model" of a single row invites a
        # full corpus re-embed to fix one vector. Systematic and isolated get
        # different sentences now. (`embeddings`/`mind` seats, 2026-08-02: an
        # extreme-value gate degrades with n, and fails-safe is not the same as works.)
        if rate >= fraction_limit:
            return 2, (f"PROVENANCE MISMATCH — SYSTEMATIC. {len(bad)} of {n} rows "
                       f"({100 * rate:.1f}%) fall below {marginal_floor}, worst "
                       f"{worst:.6f}. At that rate this is the corpus, not a few "
                       f"rows: these vectors were not written by the configured "
                       f"model, whatever `embedding_model` claims.")
        return 2, (f"PROVENANCE MISMATCH — ISOLATED. {len(bad)} of {n} rows "
                   f"({100 * rate:.1f}%, under the {100 * fraction_limit:.0f}% "
                   f"systematic bar) fall below {marginal_floor}, worst {worst:.6f}: "
                   + ", ".join(k for k, _ in bad[:8]) +
                   f". Those specific vectors are suspect and worth replacing. This "
                   f"is NOT evidence the corpus is wrong — do not re-embed "
                   f"everything to fix a handful of rows. Re-run with a larger "
                   f"--sample to see whether the rate holds.")
    if marg and len(marg) / n > fraction_limit:
        worst = min(c for _, c in marg)
        return 3, (f"THRESHOLD MISCALIBRATED FOR THIS STACK — {len(marg)} of {n} "
                   f"rows ({100 * len(marg) / n:.1f}%) sit between {marginal_floor} "
                   f"and {threshold} (worst {worst:.6f}), and none below "
                   f"{marginal_floor}. That pattern is a threshold too tight for "
                   f"this provider, NOT a corrupt corpus. Re-derive the floor here "
                   f"with memo-calibrate-drift before treating it as a finding.")
    if marg:
        worst = min(c for _, c in marg)
        return 0, (f"CONFIRMED WITH MARGINALS — {len(marg)} of {n} rows fell just "
                   f"under {threshold} (worst {worst:.6f}) but stayed above "
                   f"{marginal_floor}. A row near the line is evidence the "
                   f"threshold is near the noise, not that the model is wrong.")
    return 0, "provenance VERIFIED by measurement (not by the declared column)"


def _cosine(a, b) -> float:
    dot = sum(x * y for x, y in zip(a, b))
    na = math.sqrt(sum(x * x for x in a))
    nb = math.sqrt(sum(y * y for y in b))
    return dot / (na * nb) if na and nb else 0.0


async def _verify_document_embeddings(conn, args) -> tuple[int, int, float]:
    """The WHOLE-DOCUMENT vector table — the one this script never looked at.

    ⚠️ HOW THIS GAP HAPPENED, because it is the instructive part. This script was
    written to catch a corpus whose `document_chunks.embedding_model` column lied,
    so it sampled `document_chunks` / `chunk_embeddings` and stopped there. It then
    ran green, repeatedly, while `document_embeddings` — a table it does not read —
    served the path that had regressed from 76.9% to 43.4% rank-1.

    ⇒ A check that passes tells you only about what it sampled. `document_chunks`
    and `document_embeddings` are written by DIFFERENT code paths
    (`passages.py:138` via embed_batch, `main.py`/`backfill.py:361` via embed), so
    one being sound was never evidence about the other. The scope of a green result
    is the scope of its query, and nothing here announced that scope.

    Same technique as the chunk pass, different table: re-embed the document's own
    stored content as a SINGLETON and compare to the stored whole-document vector.
    Verified by measurement on 2026-08-02 — 25/25 across all five token bands,
    negative control 0.2268 — which is how the misaligned-vector hypothesis for the
    regression was refuted.
    """
    rows = conn.execute(
        "SELECT id, content FROM documents "
        "WHERE valid_until IS NULL AND length(coalesce(content,'')) > 0 "
        "ORDER BY created_at").fetchall()
    if not rows:
        print("\nno documents indexed — nothing to verify in document_embeddings")
        return 0, 0, 1.0

    total = len(rows)
    step = max(1, total // max(1, args.sample))
    rows = rows[::step][:args.sample]
    print(f"\n--- document_embeddings (whole-document vectors) ---")
    print(f"sampled    : {len(rows)} of {total} documents, stride {step} "
          f"(stratified by created_at)")
    print(f"detects    : any contiguous block ≥ {step} rows "
          f"(≥ {100 * step / max(1, total):.2f}% of the corpus), deterministically")

    scored: list[tuple[str, float]] = []
    worst, checked, missing = 1.0, 0, 0
    for doc_id, content in rows:
        blob = conn.execute(
            "SELECT embedding FROM document_embeddings WHERE doc_id=?",
            (doc_id,)).fetchone()
        if not blob:
            # NOT fatal on its own: a document can legitimately predate the vector
            # table or have been skipped. It IS reported, because a large count is
            # the signature of a partial backfill, which is exactly the failure the
            # stride sampling exists to catch.
            missing += 1
            print(f"  {doc_id[:8]}  NO VECTOR")
            continue
        stored = struct.unpack(f"{len(blob[0]) // 4}f", blob[0])
        # ⛔ BARE, for the same reason as the chunk pass above — see that comment.
        # A document wrongly routed through a future embed_query() would store a
        # PREFIXED vector, and only a bare re-embed here disagrees with it.
        fresh = await embeddings.embed_document(content)
        if len(stored) != len(fresh):
            print(f"  {doc_id[:8]}: width {len(stored)} stored vs {len(fresh)} fresh",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 2, checked, 0.0
        c = _cosine(stored, fresh)
        worst = min(worst, c)
        checked += 1
        scored.append((doc_id[:8], c))
        kind = classify(c, args.threshold, args.marginal_floor)
        flag = "" if kind == "ok" else f"   <-- {kind.upper()}"
        print(f"  {doc_id[:8]}  cosine={c:.6f}{flag}")

    # Its OWN negative control. The chunk pass's control does not license this one:
    # different table, different writer, different vector width in principle.
    if len(rows) >= 2:
        blob = conn.execute(
            "SELECT embedding FROM document_embeddings WHERE doc_id=?",
            (rows[0][0],)).fetchone()
        if blob:
            stored_a = struct.unpack(f"{len(blob[0]) // 4}f", blob[0])
            control = _cosine(stored_a, await embeddings.embed_document(rows[-1][1]))
            print(f"negative control (mismatched pair): cosine={control:.4f}")
            if control >= args.threshold:
                print("NEGATIVE CONTROL FAILED on document_embeddings — the "
                      "comparison returns ~1.0 to unrelated text, so every pass "
                      "above is meaningless.", file=sys.stderr)
                return 2, checked, worst

    if missing:
        print(f"  ⚠️ {missing} of {len(rows)} sampled documents have NO whole-document "
              f"vector — a partial backfill would look exactly like this")
    rc, sentence = verdict(scored, args.threshold, args.marginal_floor)
    print(f"document_embeddings: {sentence}", file=sys.stderr if rc else sys.stdout)
    return rc, checked, worst


async def main() -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__,
                                 formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    # Default 100: with stride sampling, n is the GUARANTEE, not a confidence
    # level — n ≥ 1/x deterministically catches any contiguous block of fraction
    # x. n=100 → ~1%. That is ~100 embeddings, which is nothing for a weekly
    # sweep. Drop it for a quick ad-hoc check, but read the detection limit the
    # run prints rather than assuming a small n means much.
    ap.add_argument("--sample", type=int, default=100)
    ap.add_argument("--threshold", type=float, default=THRESHOLD)
    ap.add_argument("--marginal-floor", type=float, default=MARGINAL_FLOOR,
                    help="below THRESHOLD but above this = near the line, not a "
                         "wrong model. Set from the SIGNAL's spread on your stack "
                         "(memo-calibrate-drift), never from the control's.")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    conn = db._get_or_create_conn(db.global_path())
    declared = conn.execute(
        "SELECT DISTINCT embedding_model FROM document_chunks").fetchall()
    print(f"configured : {settings.embedding_model} @ {settings.embedding_dimensions}")
    print(f"declared   : {[d[0] for d in declared]}")

    # STRATIFIED BY WRITE TIME, not uniform random. [refinement from `embeddings`]
    #
    # Uniform sampling implicitly assumes corruption is scattered. The realistic
    # failure here is an interrupted or reconfigured run, which is CONTIGUOUS in
    # `created_at` — so spreading the draws across the write-time range finds it
    # with far fewer samples than random does.
    #
    # The arithmetic that makes this matter for a RECURRING check: to catch a 1%
    # divergence at 95% confidence, uniform sampling needs n ≈ ln(0.05)/ln(0.99)
    # ≈ 300. At n=6 the probability of missing it is 0.99^6 ≈ 94%, so a weekly
    # sweep would report `ok` every Monday against a bad 1% indefinitely. Six
    # random samples prove the corpus is not WHOLESALE in the wrong space; they
    # say almost nothing about a PARTIAL divergence.
    rows = conn.execute(
        "SELECT doc_id, chunk_index, text FROM document_chunks "
        "ORDER BY created_at").fetchall()
    if not rows:
        print("no passages indexed — nothing to verify", flush=True)
        return 0
    total = len(rows)
    step = max(1, total // max(1, args.sample))
    rows = rows[::step][:args.sample]

    # STATE THE DETECTION LIMIT. The stride IS the bound, and unlike a confidence
    # level it is exact: any contiguous block of at least `step` rows is caught
    # deterministically, and a shorter one is missed ENTIRELY — not merely
    # probably. Printing n without printing the stride invites reading a small
    # sample as broad coverage.
    print(f"sampled    : {len(rows)} of {total} chunks, stride {step} "
          f"(stratified by created_at)")
    print(f"detects    : any contiguous block ≥ {step} rows "
          f"(≥ {100 * step / max(1, total):.2f}% of the corpus), deterministically")
    print("blind spot : SCATTERED corruption. Stride sampling assumes the "
          "realistic failure — an interrupted or reconfigured run — is contiguous "
          "in write time. If that assumption is ever wrong here, this is WEAKER "
          "than uniform random sampling, not stronger.")

    chunk_scored: list[tuple[str, float]] = []
    worst = 1.0
    checked = 0
    for doc_id, idx, text in rows:
        blob = conn.execute(
            "SELECT embedding FROM chunk_embeddings WHERE doc_id=? AND chunk_index=?",
            (doc_id, idx)).fetchone()
        if not blob:
            print(f"  {doc_id[:8]}#{idx}: chunk row with NO vector — index is "
                  f"inconsistent", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        stored = struct.unpack(f"{len(blob[0]) // 4}f", blob[0])
        # ⛔ THIS RE-EMBED MUST STAY BARE. Do not "make it consistent" with the
        # query side by adding qwen3's instruct prefix here.
        #
        # qwen3 wants the prefix on QUERIES ONLY; documents stay bare. When memo
        # grows embed_query()/embed_document(), ~21 call sites get reclassified by
        # hand, and a document mistakenly routed through the query path writes a
        # PREFIXED vector into a document table. This line is the only thing that
        # catches it: a bare re-embed of the stored source text will not match a
        # prefixed stored vector, so the mismatch fires loudly.
        #
        # ⇒ Prefixing here would make the detector agree with exactly the defect it
        # exists to detect. (`embeddings` seat, 2026-08-02, raised while reviewing
        # the instruct-prefix regression.)
        fresh = await embeddings.embed_document(text)
        if len(stored) != len(fresh):
            print(f"  {doc_id[:8]}#{idx}: width {len(stored)} stored vs "
                  f"{len(fresh)} fresh", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        c = _cosine(stored, fresh)
        worst = min(worst, c)
        checked += 1
        chunk_scored.append((f"{doc_id[:8]}#{idx}", c))
        kind = classify(c, args.threshold, args.marginal_floor)
        flag = "" if kind == "ok" else f"   <-- {kind.upper()}"
        print(f"  {doc_id[:8]}#{idx}  cosine={c:.6f}{flag}")

    # NEGATIVE CONTROL. Without it, six passes prove nothing: a comparison that
    # returns ~1.0 to everything produces six identical ticks and reads as proof.
    # This pairs one chunk's stored vector against a DIFFERENT chunk's text, which
    # must score far below threshold. If it does not, the instrument is broken and
    # every pass above is meaningless. (`groton` measured 0.1230 on theirs; the
    # concrete value matters less than that it is nowhere near 1.0.)
    if len(rows) >= 2:
        blob = conn.execute(
            "SELECT embedding FROM chunk_embeddings WHERE doc_id=? AND chunk_index=?",
            (rows[0][0], rows[0][1])).fetchone()
        stored_a = struct.unpack(f"{len(blob[0]) // 4}f", blob[0])
        fresh_b = await embeddings.embed_document(rows[-1][2])
        control = _cosine(stored_a, fresh_b)
        print(f"\nnegative control (mismatched pair): cosine={control:.4f}")
        if control >= args.threshold:
            print("NEGATIVE CONTROL FAILED — the comparison returns ~1.0 to "
                  "unrelated text, so every pass above is meaningless. The "
                  "instrument is broken, not the corpus.", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2

    # BOTH vector tables, or the green light is scoped to half the corpus and does
    # not say so. document_chunks/chunk_embeddings serve the passage path;
    # document_embeddings serves the whole-document path. They are written by
    # different code and can fail independently.
    doc_rc, doc_checked, doc_worst = await _verify_document_embeddings(conn, args)

    chunk_rc, chunk_sentence = verdict(chunk_scored, args.threshold,
                                       args.marginal_floor)

    print(f"\n--- summary ---")
    print(f"chunk_embeddings    : checked {checked}, worst cosine {worst:.6f}")
    if doc_checked:
        print(f"document_embeddings : checked {doc_checked}, worst cosine {doc_worst:.6f}")
    else:
        print(f"document_embeddings : nothing checked")
    print(f"threshold           : {args.threshold}  "
          f"(marginal floor {args.marginal_floor})")
    print(f"chunk_embeddings    : {chunk_sentence}")

    # Worst rc wins, and the two failure modes are DIFFERENT DIAGNOSES that must
    # not collapse into one exit code: 2 = the corpus is wrong (vectors written by
    # another model), 3 = the THRESHOLD is wrong for this stack (a cluster of rows
    # near the line and none far below it). Reporting a miscalibrated threshold as
    # a corrupt corpus is how a tool gets a corpus re-embedded for nothing.
    rc = max(chunk_rc, doc_rc) if 2 not in (chunk_rc, doc_rc) else 2
    if rc == 0:
        print("provenance VERIFIED by measurement (not by the declared column), "
              "in BOTH vector tables")
    return rc


if __name__ == "__main__":
    rc = asyncio.run(main())
    print(f"EXIT={rc}", flush=True)
    raise SystemExit(rc)
